hi this is dr. Gregory Sadler I'm a  professor of philosophy and the  president and founder of an educational  consulting company called reason IO  where we put philosophy into practice  I've studied and taught philosophy for  over 20 years and I find that many  people run into difficulties reading  classic philosophical texts sometimes  it's the way things are said or how the  text is structured but the concepts  themselves are not always that  complicated and that's where I come in  to help students and lifelong learners  I've been producing longer lecture  videos and posting them to youtube many  viewers say they find them useful what  you're currently watching is part of a  new series of shorter videos each of  them focused on one core concept from an  important philosophical text I hope you  find it useful as well one of the  activities that Iran is engaged in in  her short work the virtue of selfishness  and indeed in her larger body of work is  engaging and criticizing and ultimately  rejecting and providing reasons for  rejecting other rival moral theories or  perspectives so what are we talking  about here we're talking about any other  approach in ethics that she considers to  be wrong handed or misguided and in  doing so this is not a particularly  innovative or unusual thing in the field  of ethics you see every major moral  theory and every major moral theorist  doing the same so utilitarians need to  be able to say for example why it is  that other things either amount to  utilitarianism just not done quite so  well or are opposed to utilitarianism  and are thereby wrong for this and this  and this this reason and Rand is doing  something quite similar in terms of her  Objectivist or rational egoist  ethics so in Chapter 1  of the virtue of selfishness the  Objectivist ethics she talks in terms of  three main let's call them families of  theories she actually calls them a each  of them a theory but we should  understand them as more like a bundle or  a bucket that that theories fall into if  they have certain characteristics and  she sketches out a certain sort of very  broad history of the development of  moral ideas this is very early on and  she also makes some rather startling  claims she says that no philosopher has  given a rational objectively  demonstrable scientific answer the  question of why man needs a code of  values so long as that question remained  unanswered no rational scientific  objective code of ethics could be  discovered or defined and then she  brings up Aristotle who she's a big fan  of the greatest of all philosophers  Aristotle did not regard ethics as an  exact science so she's she's claiming  that what she's doing is for the very  first time a truly scientific objective  type of ethics now again that's not a  radically new claim we see people like  Thomas Hobbes making that in Leviathan  and we can set that aside for the moment  but if you accept that point of view  which we're going to do for the time  being then the story that she's telling  makes quite a bit of sense so she says  that most philosophers took the  existence of ethics for granted as the  given as a historical fact they were not  concerned with discovering its  metaphysical cause or objective  validation many of them attempted to  break the traditional monopoly of what  she's calling mysticism in the field of  ethics and allegedly to define a  rational scientific non religious  morality so that's good actually going  to be stage two so what is stage one  it's what she's calling the mystic  approach or a theory or a perspective in  ethics so let's take a look at what she  actually says about that the mystics and  here she's not using the term in a very  precise sense to mean you know what we  we can label as mysticism in say  Neoplatonic philosophy or you know when  we're talking in a religious studies  sense she's using in a very broad  essentially pejorative way of talking  about anybody who's non rational and  appealing to something supernatural or  God or anything along those lines so she  says they held the arbitrary  unaccountable will of God as the  standard of the good and the validation  of their ethics so any sort of ethics  that has a religious basis whether that  be that of you know traditional  Christianity or Islam or Mahayana  Buddhism I mean if you look at people  like to say buddhism is non-theistic  that's not entirely true and and we  could talk about other you know  spiritual leaders is sort of straddling  the line think about the Confucianism  any of those sort of things Rand would  probably place in the bucket of mystical  and she says that a little bit later on  this is towards the end of the the  chapter  she says the mystic theory of ethics is  explicitly based on the premise that the  standard of value of man's ethics is set  beyond the grave so there's some sort of  heavenly reward whether it be a better  reincarnation or paradise or avoiding  Hell or who knows what it's going to be  she says that it's set by the laws or  requirements of another supernatural  dimension and she goes on a little bit  further and you know I think she's  actually getting at something that we  see some religious perspectives putting  forth so she's not entirely distorting  you know the essence of religion and  talking about this but it certainly  doesn't apply to everyone she says it  means that ethics is impossible for  human beings to practice it's unsuited  for and imposed to man's life on earth  that man must take the blame for it and  suffer through the whole of his earthly  existence to atone for the guilt of  being unable to practice the impractical  so it's saying essentially there's this  this whatever it is this transcendent  ideal that's being set up  purportedly the will of the divine and  human beings can't measure up to it but  they're nevertheless supposed to measure  up to it so they feel guilty and that's  what's driving them and it's thoroughly  irrational and she says this is what we  can associate with the Dark Ages and the  Middle Ages they are they are the  existential monument to this theory of  ethics meaning that we can look at those  time periods and their cultures and  their moral discourses and say aha  there's there's the mystical theory of  ethics so put that aside for the moment  and we come to what she calls the social  theory of ethics and she says that this  is derived from philosophers who are  trying to get past any sort of religious  trend you know transcendent supernatural  grounding of ethics and they're trying  to provide something in its place and  she calls them neo mystics because they  replace God with the good of society and  she says collapsing into the circularity  of a definition such as the standard of  the good is that which is good for  society and then she says this means  that society stands above any principle  of ethics because it's the source  standard and criterion of ethics the  good is whatever society wills and this  means that the individual has to be  subordinated and if need be sacrificed  to the demands of society which is  really the demands either of an elite  who's deciding what to do with the  social levers or a sort of vague khun's  exception or a concrete experience of  other people so the social theory of  ethics is saying that the human being  ought to be sacrificed if need be for  the good of others and again she  discusses this at the end of the chapter  here we go  saying that although it claims its chief  concern is life on Earth it's not the  life of man at the life of an individual  but the life of a disembodied entity the  collective which in relation every  individual consists of everybody except  himself as far as the individual is  concerned his ethical duty is to be the  selfless voiceless right list slave of  any need claim or demand asserted by  others so you know this this view is  that one doesn't really have any  intrinsic value or rights except in  